{{define "profile" -}}
You are deciding which of this machine's coding-agent configurations are
worth importing into the server where those agents will run. Decide from
the inventory below; it is the whole input.

{{if .RepoPath -}}
Your working directory is the git repository at {{.RepoPath}}. Read its
files to see what the project is, and prefer configuration that matches
it. Never modify, create, or delete repository files.
{{- else -}}
No repository was given. Reason from the inventories alone and do not go
looking for one.
{{- end}}

The inventory names, for each harness that has a configuration directory
on this machine, its root path, its file count and byte size, then the
same per category with the paths in that category:

<<<INVENTORY
{{.Inventory}}
INVENTORY

Never open a profile file, and never read credential stores such as
keychains, .netrc, .ssh, .aws, or browser profiles. The inventory carries
no file contents, and it leaves out every credential file and every file a
secret scanner flagged, so a path you cannot see is withheld on purpose,
not missing by accident.

Import a harness's configuration when it carries the user's own working
setup - skills, custom commands, standing instructions - that agents on
the
server would otherwise lack. Skip it when the profile holds nothing
but defaults, or when it belongs to a harness the user clearly does not
use.

Write exactly one file, profile.json, into {{.OutputDir}}:

{"harnesses":[
  {"harness":"claude","import":true,"categories":["skills","commands"],
   "reason":"one sentence a developer can check against the file list"},
  {"harness":"codex","import":false,"categories":[],"reason":"..."}
]}

The file must satisfy every rule below, or it is rejected:

1. One entry per harness the inventory names, and no others: each appears
   exactly once, whether or not you import it.
2. "categories" holds only category names from that harness's own
   inventory. It is non-empty when "import" is true and empty when
   "import" is false.
3. "reason" is one sentence on one line, under 300 characters, and states
   something a developer can check against the file list rather than
   summarizing it.
4. No other keys, and no other files.
{{- end}}
